{"id":"active-91100-opus--7c3c1848890741b99f29e1458226cec0","request_url":"/data/requests/active-91100-opus--7c3c1848890741b99f29e1458226cec0.json","request_sha256":"c40409b69e603d11c00850b179a0ca513a4d51a570d48c505e5aac2455ab2835","images":[],"thinking":"Turning from heading 225 to 270 and moving 0.75 units forward shifts me west along the x-axis, from (2.950, 7.400) to (2.200, 7.400) -- which keeps me on that same constant-x edge rather than breaking the degeneracy I intended.\n\nActually, moving perpendicular to the edge (in x) gives good parallax since the edge lies along constant x. Moving along y (parallel to the edge) would keep the observation planes nearly coincident, so translating in x toward or away from the edge seems like the right move to disambiguate its true position.\n\nFrom two camera positions along x, I can set up depth equations relating the pixel row and known camera-edge distances to solve for the edge's (x, z) coordinates. I should check the next observation file to get the actual measurements.\n\n","answer":"","tools":[{"name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/work/observations/obs_0151.png"}}],"response":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CexSUam38kRZuy5rAoCrF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Turning from heading 225 to 270 and moving 0.75 units forward shifts me west along the x-axis, from (2.950, 7.400) to (2.200, 7.400) -- which keeps me on that same constant-x edge rather than breaking the degeneracy I intended.\n\nActually, moving perpendicular to the edge (in x) gives good parallax since the edge lies along constant x. Moving along y (parallel to the edge) would keep the observation planes nearly coincident, so translating in x toward or away from the edge seems like the right move to disambiguate its true position.\n\nFrom two camera positions along x, I can set up depth equations relating the pixel row and known camera-edge distances to solve for the edge's (x, z) coordinates. I should c